# Branch Policy

GitHub’s default branch is `main` and stays `main`.

All work lands on **`dev`** (direct push is allowed). Do not commit, cherry-pick,
rebase, or open PRs that change **`main`**. Do not use GitHub Merge / squash /
rebase onto `main`.

The **only** allowed update to `main` is a **fast-forward from `dev`**, and
only to publish a **product release** (`release(X.Y.Z)`) or a **hotfix** of
that release:

```text
git fetch origin
git checkout main
git merge --ff-only origin/dev
git push origin main
```

If `--ff-only` refuses, stop and ask. Do not create a merge commit on `main`.
Never force-push `main`. Never hard-reset `dev` to hide divergence; ask instead.

A hotfix may need a branch off `main`. The developer may also create a
branch off `dev`. Both are exceptional; do not design that layout ahead of
time. When it happens, stop and ask how to branch, merge, and name it.
